---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import Breadcrumbs from "../../components/Breadcrumbs.astro";

const title = "Hicks v. Stahl: Summary Withdrawn";
const canonicalUrl = "https://www.section1983.org/cases/hicks-v-stahl/";
const crumbs = [
  { label: "Home", href: "/" },
  { label: "Case Law Library", href: "/cases/" },
  { label: "Summary correction" },
];
---

<Base
  title={title}
  description="The previous civil-case summary was withdrawn because its source and account could not be verified."
  canonicalUrl={canonicalUrl}
>
  <Breadcrumbs crumbs={crumbs} />
  <article class="prose prose-slate max-w-none">
    <h1 class="text-3xl font-bold text-navy mb-6">{title}</h1>
    <p>
      The previous summary has been withdrawn pending verification of the
      original civil opinion. We have been unable to substantiate the decision
      date, factual account, or disposition previously attributed to No.
      3:11-CV-02703-P. Treat that summary as unsupported authority.
    </p>
    <p>
      The former page also overstated the effect of a criminal conviction. Under{" "}
      <a href="/cases/heck-v-humphrey/">Heck v. Humphrey</a>, the judge asks{" "}
      whether success on the particular civil claim would necessarily show that
      an existing conviction or sentence is invalid. A conviction leaves room
      for claims that can succeed while the conviction remains valid.
    </p>
    <p>
      The former discussion of Texas Tort Claims Act § 101.106 also requires
      correction. Subsection (e) provides for dismissal of covered state tort
      claims against employees on the governmental unit's motion when both are
      sued under that Act. Its application depends on the claims and filing
      arrangement. Read the{" "}
      <a href="https://statutes.capitol.texas.gov/docs/CP/htm/CP.101.htm#101.106">
        statutory text
      </a>{" "}
      separately from the rules governing federal claims.
    </p>
    <p>
      Use the{" "}
      <a href="/articles/heck-when-a-section-1983-claim-is-barred/">
        Heck guide
      </a>{" "}
      and <a href="/cases/">case library</a> to continue your research with
      identifiable sources.
    </p>
  </article>
</Base>
